The problem

Suppose you have:

interface PaymentService {
    void pay();
}

Two implementations:

@Service
public class UpiPaymentService implements PaymentService {
    // ...
}

@Service
public class CardPaymentService implements PaymentService {
    // ...
}

Now Spring sees:

@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final PaymentService paymentService;

    public OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {
        this.paymentService = paymentService;
    }
}

Spring asks:
"Which PaymentService should I inject? There are two!"

This causes:
NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException

@Primary

You can tell Spring:

"Use this implementation by default."

Ex:
@Service
@Primary
public class UpiPaymentService implements PaymentService {
}

Now:

public OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {
    this.paymentService = paymentService;
}

Spring chooses:

PaymentService
      │
      ├── UpiPaymentService  ← @Primary ⭐
      │
      └── CardPaymentService

So @Primary means:

When multiple beans match, prefer this bean by default.

Note :
Even if UpiPaymentService is @Primary, the explicit @Qualifier selection takes precedence.

Summary:

@Primary
   ↓
"If nobody specifies, choose me."

@Qualifier
   ↓
"I specifically want this one."
